{"rule": "Return this JSON object with the rule field unchanged and a new passage of about 250 words in the text field.", "text": "The twine gave way easily, as though it had been waiting. Mira folded back the oilcloth, and the smell of salt grew stronger, sharp enough to sting. Tobias clicked on the flashlight and angled the beam into the crate. Inside lay a bundle of letters, their envelopes yellowed and soft as cloth, and beneath them a brass key no longer than her finger. At the very bottom, nestled in a bed of dried seaweed, was a single shell, pale pink and curled like an ear. \"She said she never went to the coast,\" Mira whispered. Tobias lifted the top envelope and turned it toward the light. The handwriting was elegant, slanted, unmistakably her grandmother's. It was addressed to someone named Elias, care of the keeper's cottage at Harrow Point. There was no stamp. \"She never sent it,\" he said. Mira took the letter from him with trembling hands. The flap had been sealed once, then carefully opened and resealed, the wax cracked down the middle. She counted the envelopes. Eleven. Every one was addressed to Elias, and not one had been mailed. The rain eased, and in the sudden hush she could hear the clock downstairs ticking toward midnight. Tobias picked up the brass key and held it to the flashlight. Etched along its shaft, so faint she almost missed it, were three small letters: H.P.L. \"Harrow Point Lighthouse,\" Mira said. She pressed the shell to her ear without thinking. Beneath the hush of the ocean, she could have sworn she heard someone calling her name."}
